it through the diastatic

507
00:22:35,670 --> 00:22:39,350
portion but then use
the umbilical ligament

508
00:22:39,390 --> 00:22:42,470
to flap over
the mesh and to help

509
00:22:42,510 --> 00:22:43,990
sandwich that portion
of the mesh

510
00:22:44,030 --> 00:22:45,390
that has
not been sutured

511
00:22:45,430 --> 00:22:47,010
to the underside
of the abdominal wall.

512
00:22:47,270 --> 00:22:49,730
And so that mesh
gets integrated

513
00:22:49,770 --> 00:22:51,870
because it's sandwiched
between the layers

514
00:22:51,910 --> 00:22:55,590
of the falcipar ligament
and linea alba.

515
00:23:01,010 --> 00:23:03,000
So over the years,
I think many surgeons

516
00:23:03,040 --> 00:23:06,040
have really learned
much more about

517
00:23:06,080 --> 00:23:07,900
the retrorectus space
than we knew,

518
00:23:07,940 --> 00:23:10,560
I would say,
15 years ago.

519
00:23:10,780 --> 00:23:13,080
We now know
that in the upper third

520
00:23:13,120 --> 00:23:14,620
of the retrorectus space,

521
00:23:14,820 --> 00:23:17,580
the neurovascular
fibers are quite medial.

522
00:23:17,620 --> 00:23:21,440
So when we dissect
the retrorectus space,

523
00:23:21,660 --> 00:23:23,920
we're in upper third,
we actually cheat

524
00:23:23,960 --> 00:23:26,880
a little bit towards
the midline and especially

525
00:23:26,920 --> 00:23:29,740
when the transverse
abdominis muscle fibers,

526
00:23:29,880 --> 00:23:32,580
where we see kind
of this real actual

527
00:23:32,620 --> 00:23:34,860
transverse abdominis muscle
fibers, which you see

528
00:23:34,900 --> 00:23:37,720
in the upper third
versus down below,

529
00:23:37,780 --> 00:23:38,880
you only see
the aponeurosis.

530
00:23:38,920 --> 00:23:41,200
Those are
the locations where you're

531
00:23:41,240 --> 00:23:42,420
typically going
to see the nerves.

532
00:23:42,740 --> 00:23:45,200
And this is,
when talking about transversus

533
00:23:45,240 --> 00:23:46,980
abdominis muscle release,
this is what's referred

534
00:23:47,020 --> 00:23:50,120
to a lot of times
as TAR modification

535
00:23:50,160 --> 00:23:53,880
or Madrid TAR modification,
where the surgeons

536
00:23:53,920 --> 00:23:58,260
from Madrid actually completely
leave that space unviolated.

537
00:23:58,360 --> 00:24:00,080
We still open
the posterior rectus sheath,

538
00:24:00,180 --> 00:24:02,020
but we just stay
quite medial,

539
00:24:02,100 --> 00:24:04,240
so you don't get
the same mesh overlap.

540
00:24:04,280 --> 00:24:06,720
a mesh at that point
is actually running

541
00:24:06,760 --> 00:24:10,980
off and this way
not disturbing those neurovascular

542
00:24:11,020 --> 00:24:15,840
fibers otherwise I think
if you go ahead

543
00:24:15,880 --> 00:24:20,100
and respect those neurofibers
the closure still happens

544
00:24:20,140 --> 00:24:23,560
the same you know
we go from subxiphoid

545
00:24:23,600 --> 00:24:26,300
space all the way
down to the arcate

546
00:24:26,340 --> 00:24:27,960
line closing
the posterior layer.

547
00:24:28,600 --> 00:24:32,460
We have had good
success without seeing any

548
00:24:32,500 --> 00:24:35,420
atrophy in the upper
third of the rectus

549
00:24:35,460 --> 00:24:38,860
abdominis muscle as I think
many of us

550
00:24:38,900 --> 00:24:42,260
have seen early
on in our experience

551
00:24:42,300 --> 00:24:44,620
with transversus abdominis muscle
release and maybe some

552
00:24:44,660 --> 00:24:49,040
of the retrorectus
dissections where perhaps

553
00:24:49,080 --> 00:24:54,140
The medial fibers
of the neurovascular bundles have

554
00:24:54,180 --> 00:24:57,280
not been identified
as well

555
00:24:57,320 --> 00:25:00,400
as we identify
them right now.

556
00:25:05,820 --> 00:25:06,520
Answering this question,

557
00:25:06,560 --> 00:25:07,940
I think I want
to be very careful.

558
00:25:08,080 --> 00:25:11,800
I have a great
concern promoting posterior

559
00:25:11,840 --> 00:25:15,160
rectus sheath closure
because I do think it's

560
00:25:15,200 --> 00:25:18,280
an extremely technical
step where I spend

561
00:25:18,320 --> 00:25:21,520
a lot of time
now very carefully closing

562
00:25:21,560 --> 00:25:22,580
this posterior layer.

563
00:25:22,620 --> 00:25:24,720
This posterior layer closure
can take me up

564
00:25:24,760 --> 00:25:27,700
to sometimes 40-45 minutes
to close well.

565
00:25:28,370 --> 00:25:29,620
And I do this all
the time.

566
00:25:30,300 --> 00:25:33,600
So with that in mind,
I think, again,

567
00:25:33,680 --> 00:25:36,440
it's a major step
of this procedure.

568
00:25:37,070 --> 00:25:37,800
So you can't,

569
00:25:37,840 --> 00:25:39,800
I don't think
you should rush through this.

570
00:25:40,500 --> 00:25:41,420
And when I answer
this question,

571
00:25:41,620 --> 00:25:42,720
there's a lot
of experience

572
00:25:42,760 --> 00:25:46,220
that have went through
closing this layer

573
00:25:46,260 --> 00:25:48,140
and learning how
to close this layer.

574
00:25:48,180 --> 00:25:52,840
Yes, I have seen
a few disruptions early

575
00:25:52,880 --> 00:25:55,880
on, mostly
in the upper aspect

576
00:25:55,920 --> 00:25:59,000
of the posterior
rectus sheath,

577
00:25:59,060 --> 00:26:02,820
especially around the ribcage
where there is perhaps

578
00:26:02,860 --> 00:26:05,220
the most tension
of the posterior layer.

579
00:26:05,260 --> 00:26:08,760
Otherwise, more recently,
in the last few years,

580
00:26:08,980 --> 00:26:11,720
really the only posterior
layer disruptions that I've

581
00:26:11,760 --> 00:26:13,980
seen is actually
the most recent one

582
00:26:14,020 --> 00:26:17,200
I've seen in my practice
was a disruption

583
00:26:17,240 --> 00:26:20,680
of the umbilical ligaments
below the arcate line.

584
00:26:21,350 --> 00:26:23,220
That layer was
not closed well where

585
00:26:23,260 --> 00:26:25,400
we had the umbilical
ligament pull away

586
00:26:25,440 --> 00:26:27,800
from the arcate line
and the contents

587
00:26:27,840 --> 00:26:32,060
of intra-abdominal contents actually
slipped into that space.

588
00:26:32,100 --> 00:26:33,420
In this case,
that has happened,

589
00:26:33,480 --> 00:26:35,340
but the posterior layer,
more recently,

590
00:26:35,460 --> 00:26:37,880
has been intact,
and I think we've had

591
00:26:37,920 --> 00:26:39,880
pretty good success
with the closure.

592
00:26:40,880 --> 00:26:42,100
You know,
there are many steps.

593
00:26:42,340 --> 00:26:43,340
There's how
you close it,

594
00:26:43,380 --> 00:26:44,880
but also extremely important,

595
00:26:45,140 --> 00:26:47,640
how the anesthesiologist
wakes the patients up.

596
00:26:47,760 --> 00:26:51,580
If the patient is
bucking, the highest risk

597
00:26:51,620 --> 00:26:54,120
in this procedure
for your work

598
00:26:54,160 --> 00:26:56,520
to come apart is
during the wake-up.

599
00:26:56,560 --> 00:27:00,300
We all hold pressure
on the belly

600
00:27:00,340 --> 00:27:01,460
as the patient
is waking up

601
00:27:01,500 --> 00:27:03,400
and our anesthesiologists
who are working

602
00:27:03,440 --> 00:27:04,860
with us are
very experienced.

603
00:27:05,490 --> 00:27:07,020
A lot of times
when safe,

604
00:27:07,240 --> 00:27:09,820
we do deep extubations
so the patient does

605
00:27:09,860 --> 00:27:11,520
not generate
this intra-optimal pressure

606
00:27:11,560 --> 00:27:14,860
and potentially rip
the repair.

607
00:27:15,660 --> 00:27:18,280
If the repair comes
undone, it's very audible.

608
00:27:18,520 --> 00:27:20,480
You can see
popping sounds

609
00:27:20,520 --> 00:27:23,960
of stitches popping through
or actually breaking.

610
00:27:25,200 --> 00:27:26,700
When you hear
this once,

611
00:27:26,740 --> 00:27:28,160
you'll always remember
it, and especially

612
00:27:28,200 --> 00:27:29,620
if you have a hand
on the belly,

613
00:27:29,720 --> 00:27:32,860
you'll feel the vibration
of the stitch breaking,

614
00:27:33,100 --> 00:27:34,320
and in those cases,

615
00:27:34,360 --> 00:27:36,520
I think the most
prudent thing is

616
00:27:36,560 --> 00:27:38,240
to put the patient
back to sleep

617
00:27:38,280 --> 00:27:40,200
and go back
and redo the repair.

618
00:27:40,380 --> 00:27:41,980
It's very discouraging
when that happens.

619
00:27:42,060 --> 00:27:45,420
Has it happened?
Yes, but it hasn't

620
00:27:45,460 --> 00:27:47,260
happened to us
in a few years.

621
00:27:47,300 --> 00:27:52,920
A lot

622
00:27:52,960 --> 00:27:55,620
of those patients early
on were getting preoperative

623
00:27:55,660 --> 00:27:57,020
and postoperative CT scans.

624
00:27:58,030 --> 00:27:59,420
There were a number
of patients who would

625
00:27:59,460 --> 00:28:02,340
present to us
if there were any issues.

626
00:28:02,420 --> 00:28:05,040
We were pre-liberal
getting CT scans,

627
00:28:05,200 --> 00:28:06,200
evaluating those patients,

628
00:28:06,340 --> 00:28:08,360
and making sure
the posterior layer is intact.

629
00:28:09,340 --> 00:28:12,280
Now, as far
as our standard practice,

630
00:28:12,480 --> 00:28:14,040
this is not something
we perform

631
00:28:14,080 --> 00:28:16,840
with all the patients.
But again,

632
00:28:16,920 --> 00:28:18,520
we do this in high
volume now.

633
00:28:18,560 --> 00:28:20,700
Now, we know there
are outcomes,

634
00:28:20,940 --> 00:28:24,960
but the studies that were
published more recently

635
00:28:25,000 --> 00:28:28,840
were able to identify
patients who had CT

636
00:28:28,880 --> 00:28:33,120
scans beforehand and had
CT scans afterwards

637
00:28:33,160 --> 00:28:34,520
for one reason
or another,

638
00:28:34,760 --> 00:28:37,460
and we compared
the CT scans from before

639
00:28:37,500 --> 00:28:39,740
and after,
and the report about

640
00:28:39,780 --> 00:28:43,720
the morphological changes was
from patients

641
00:28:43,760 --> 00:28:46,040
who had before
and after CT scans.

642
00:28:46,080 --> 00:28:49,200
At this point now,
in elective practice,

643
00:28:49,480 --> 00:28:54,620
we typically will see
patients initially at about

644
00:28:54,660 --> 00:28:56,700
three to five days
after surgery to remove

645
00:28:56,740 --> 00:29:00,580
the retromuscular drain,
and then about four weeks

646
00:29:00,620 --> 00:29:02,980
to five weeks afterwards
just to make sure

647
00:29:03,020 --> 00:29:06,400
that patients are doing
well, examine the abdomen.

648
00:29:06,440 --> 00:29:08,040
if there's any questions
we can get

649
00:29:08,080 --> 00:29:09,980
a post-operative CT scan
specifically looking

650
00:29:10,020 --> 00:29:12,640
at the repair side
and then otherwise

651
00:29:12,680 --> 00:29:15,060
we give the patient
option to follow up

652
00:29:15,100 --> 00:29:16,960
with us at six
months and 12 months

653
00:29:17,000 --> 00:29:19,320
afterwards and in
the past we used

654
00:29:19,360 --> 00:29:21,320
to see them
at 24 months as well.

655
00:29:26,730 --> 00:29:30,370
I think if you teach
surgeons appropriately how

656
00:29:30,410 --> 00:29:32,410
to do this of course
I think surgeons

657
00:29:32,450 --> 00:29:35,110
can close this and having
the appropriate tools.

658
00:29:35,410 --> 00:29:39,030
I do think using
the robot is something

659
00:29:39,070 --> 00:29:42,570
of an advantage
in this cases and being

660
00:29:42,610 --> 00:29:44,710
able to reproduce
the complex closure here

661
00:29:44,750 --> 00:29:47,510
as opposed to doing
this laparoscopic.

662
00:29:47,590 --> 00:29:50,570
Laparoscopically this is technically
quite challenging to perform.

663
00:29:56,030 --> 00:29:58,090
I think you would
need the before

664
00:29:58,130 --> 00:30:00,450
and after CT scans
at least after CT

665
00:30:00,490 --> 00:30:02,630
scan specifically looking
at posterior layer.

666
00:30:03,350 --> 00:30:06,810
There is some findings
when the posterior layer

667
00:30:06,850 --> 00:30:09,170
comes apart, which are
very obvious on CT

668
00:30:09,210 --> 00:30:11,530
scan that we can
separately talk about.

669
00:30:12,150 --> 00:30:13,750
But that type
of study would settle

670
00:30:13,790 --> 00:30:17,190
and I would get
those CT scans about

671
00:30:17,230 --> 00:30:19,270
six months afterwards
and about

672
00:30:19,310 --> 00:30:21,850
as well as two
years afterwards.

673
00:30:22,030 --> 00:30:23,010
I think
in my opinion,

674
00:30:23,050 --> 00:30:25,650
once the mesh sets
in place and kind

675
00:30:25,690 --> 00:30:28,170
of holds everything
together, long term,

676
00:30:28,250 --> 00:30:30,610
that posterior layer is
It's unlikely to come

677
00:30:30,650 --> 00:30:33,250
apart where things can
slip in there anymore

678
00:30:33,290 --> 00:30:36,810
because, again,
the mesh integrates

679
00:30:36,850 --> 00:30:38,970
into the posterior layer
and the underside

680
00:30:39,010 --> 00:30:40,450
of the rectus
abdominis muscle.

681
00:30:40,730 --> 00:30:42,750
Most of those failures,
so if not all

682
00:30:42,790 --> 00:30:45,510
of those failures, happen
in early postoperative period,

683
00:30:45,590 --> 00:30:47,650
if not immediately
during the wake-up.

684
00:30:47,790 --> 00:30:49,150
That is an important
thing to consider.

685
00:30:49,190 --> 00:30:52,050
and long term
if say you get

686
00:30:52,090 --> 00:30:54,550
a CT scan about
eight weeks after surgery

687
00:30:54,590 --> 00:30:57,250
and everything is intact
it is my belief

688
00:30:57,290 --> 00:30:59,410
that it is unlikely
to come apart

689
00:30:59,450 --> 00:31:02,310
and it is unlikely
to develop an interparietal

690
00:31:02,350 --> 00:31:06,010
hernia especially if you really
address the whole

691
00:31:06,050 --> 00:31:08,810
retrorectus space
with the mesh from subxiphoid

692
00:31:08,850 --> 00:31:10,270
space all the way
down to the space

693
00:31:10,310 --> 00:31:11,770
of Retzius which is
close to the posterior

694
00:31:11,810 --> 00:31:13,910
sheath It's unlikely
at that point

695
00:31:13,950 --> 00:31:16,410
to come apart
in the long term.

696
00:31:21,800 --> 00:31:24,040
At this point,
I feel like consistently

697
00:31:24,080 --> 00:31:26,380
when I give a talk
about eTEP access

698
00:31:26,420 --> 00:31:29,060
surgery, I try
to give a word of,

699
00:31:29,160 --> 00:31:32,420
not so much warning,
but really I think

700
00:31:32,460 --> 00:31:35,460
surgeons should try
to hesitate and not rush

701
00:31:35,500 --> 00:31:38,980
into performing eTEP
access procedure.

702
00:31:39,020 --> 00:31:41,620
You should really
ask yourself,

703
00:31:41,920 --> 00:31:44,740
what is the reason
you want

704
00:31:44,780 --> 00:31:46,220
to use
the retrorectus space?

705
00:31:47,420 --> 00:31:51,100
Why certain patients
deserve retrorectus repairs

706
00:31:51,140 --> 00:31:53,580
and why certain patients,
their problems

707
00:31:53,620 --> 00:31:55,520
may be addressed
with a simple repair.

708
00:31:55,560 --> 00:31:58,400
In fact, I would
argue that most

709
00:31:58,440 --> 00:32:01,440
of the patients out
there can be addressed

710
00:32:01,480 --> 00:32:04,960
with a simple repair,
such as robotic TAP

711
00:32:05,000 --> 00:32:08,740
repair or laparoscopic TAP,
ventral hernia repair.

712
00:32:09,560 --> 00:32:11,720
There is
a P-TEP repair,

713
00:32:11,780 --> 00:32:13,480
it's a little bit
more advanced repairs,

714
00:32:13,660 --> 00:32:17,100
but still don't violate
the retrorectus space.

715
00:32:17,140 --> 00:32:19,800
As well as, I think,
IPOM procedures,

716
00:32:19,860 --> 00:32:23,360
when done well, understanding
the technical aspects

717
00:32:23,400 --> 00:32:28,020
and places where
those repairs can fail,

718
00:32:28,280 --> 00:32:29,760
if you understand
this well,

719
00:32:29,800 --> 00:32:33,160
you can still
do great work doing IPOM

720
00:32:33,200 --> 00:32:35,940
procedures with closure
or defects without having

721
00:32:35,980 --> 00:32:39,580
to use the advanced
techniques like eTEP access,

722
00:32:39,800 --> 00:32:41,560
or you do retrorectus
repair,

723
00:32:41,780 --> 00:32:44,660
or you do a transversus
abdominis muscle release.

724
00:32:44,700 --> 00:32:47,000
At the end
of the day,

725
00:32:47,040 --> 00:32:52,860
each patient deserves
a certain unique

726
00:32:52,900 --> 00:32:55,060
procedure, unique
to the problem

727
00:32:55,100 --> 00:32:56,700
they are presented
to you with.

728
00:32:56,980 --> 00:32:57,980
Hope this helps.
